<title>LUXEMBOURG: Court upholds state aid to Siemens illegal.</title>
<headline>Court upholds state aid to Siemens illegal.</headline>
<dateline>LUXEMBOURG 1997-05-15</dateline>
<text>
<p>The European Court of Justice rejected on Thursday an appeal from Siemens against the European Commission's decision that monies it received from the Brussels Regional Authorities were illegal state aid.</p>
<p>The Commission found the aid incompatible with the common market and ordered Belgium to recover monies already paid and to cancel any further payments.</p>
<p>The court confirmed the Court of First Instance's holding that the Commission did provide an adequate reasoning for its decision and that the aid provided did not finance investments in tangible or intangible assets operations as required under the Belgian scheme.</p>
<p>***</p>
<p>EXCERPTS FROM THE JUDGMENT</p>
<p>C-278/95 P</p>
<p>&quot;Appeal - state aid - General aid - Definition of aid&quot;</p>
<p>Siemens SA, appellant</p>
<p>appeal against the judgment of the The European Court of First Instance in Case T-459/93, seeking to have that judgment set aside</p>
<p>the other party to the proceedings being</p>
<p>Commission of the European Communities</p>
<p>---</p>
<p>THE COURT hereby rules</p>
<p>1. Dismisses the appeal;</p>
<p>2. Orders Siemens to pay the costs.</p>
</text>
